GAL22V10
speechc

CLK  MMAP  RO   RI     M1      M0       RESET    READY   INT    RS     WS      GND
NC   INH   ARM  BUSY   STATEO  STATUSI  STATUSO  WSRST   RSRST  WSCLK  RSCLK   VCC


; Variant for the TMS5220C. Identical to the TMS5220 variant except that it
; implements the hardware reset at $FE_3. Do NOT use this variant with an
; original TMS5220: asserting /WS and /RS together is undefined for that chip.

; Memory Map Lines
;
;   Function | M1 | M0
;  ----------+----+----
;   DATA_IN  |  0 |  0
;   STATUS   |  0 |  1
;   STATE    |  1 |  0
;   RESET    |  1 |  1

;
; Active Low Input lines
;   RO
;   RI
;   RESET
;   READY
;   INT
;   MMAP
;
; Active Low Output Lines
;   STATEO
;   STATUSO
;   WSRST
;   RSRST
;
; ARM (pin 15) is an internal state bit. The pin is not connected on the board.
;


;
; Pin Logic TMS5220C Controller
;

;
; Write Data for External Speech Data, and start of TMS5220C reset
;

; The WSCLK pin should go high on the CLK when the conditions to intiate a write
; operation are present. When the clock goes low, the WSCLK should be reset.
;
; The second term starts a TMS5220C reset: a write to $FE_3 while the board is
; not busy clocks the WS and RS flip flops together, so /WS and /RS are both
; asserted to the TMS5220C.
WSCLK.R = /MMAP * /RI * RO * /M1 * /M0 * /BUSY + /MMAP * /RI * RO * M1 * M0 * /BUSY


; READY should go high within 100 ns of WS. That is a 10 MHz clock, which
; is faster than CLK. WSCLK should be active for long enough to
; prevent WSRST from being activated in the time between WS being activated
; and READY going high. Given that, the WSRST should be activated when
; WSCLK is not active, WS is active, and READY is not active (should have transitioned
; from HIGH to LOW given WS was active)
;
; The /RS qualifier keeps READY from ending a reset early. A reset is ended by
; the third term: a second write to $FE_3 once ARM is set.

/WSRST.R = /WSCLK * WS * /READY * /RS + /RESET + ARM * /MMAP * /RI * RO * M1 * M0

;
; TMS5220C Status Fetch Operation
;


; activate RS (active low) when memory map is appropriate and active RI and
; speech module not otherwise busy, and keep active while READY
; is high. Assumes READY will go high within 1 clock cycle.
;
; The second term is the reset start (see WSCLK).
RSCLK.R = /MMAP * /RI * RO * /M1 * M0 * /BUSY + /MMAP * /RI * RO * M1 * M0 * /BUSY

; The /WS qualifier keeps READY from ending a reset early. The second term
; releases /RS one clock after /WS at the end of a reset, regardless of READY,
; so /WS is never left asserted on its own (which the TMS5220C would take as a
; data write). /RS on its own is only a status read.
STATUSI.R = /RSCLK * RS * /READY * /WS + ARM * RS * /WS

/RSRST = STATUSI + /RESET

;
; TMS5220C Reset
;
; The TMS5220C resets when /WS and /RS are asserted at the same time. The
; sequence is timed by the host:
;
;   1. Write any value to $FE_3 while BUSY is low. WS and RS are both set.
;   2. Wait for the reset hold time.
;   3. Write to $FE_3 again. WS is cleared on that clock and RS one clock later.
;   4. Poll BUSY until it is low.
;
; ARM is set once the write strobe of step 1 has ended with both flip flops
; set, so that a strobe spanning more than one clock cannot end the reset it
; started. ARM clears on the clock after either flip flop clears.

ARM.R = WS * RS * RI + ARM * WS * RS


; Write status register to data bus when memory map is appropriate and active RO
; and speech module not otherwise busy.
 /STATUSO = /MMAP * RI * /RO * /M1 * M0 * /BUSY * STATEO

;
; Speech Board State
;

; Write board state to data bus when memory map is appropriate,
/STATEO = /MMAP * RI * /RO * M1 * /M0 * STATUSO

;
; Speech Module Busy
;

BUSY = RS + STATUSI + WS + READY

; Set the INH line to disable host computer RAM and ROM when
; ever the speech board is going to write to the data bus

INH = /MMAP * RI * /RO * /M1 * M0 * /BUSY * STATEO + /MMAP * RI * /RO * M1 * /M0 * STATUSO

DESCRIPTION

Glue logic to connect TMS5220C to 8 bit data bus and controlled via memory map.
This variant is for the TMS5220C and implements the hardware reset at $FE_3.
